= Late May 2025 — The last reading before the count

The published poll carries a **±1.6 point working margin** and is built from the
same thing the ballot will be: retained first-round votes, partial transfers,
abstention, and whatever new turnout each camp can manufacture in fourteen days.

<div class="poll-table">
<div class="poll-row"><span><b>[+ pres_2025_runoff_a_name +]</b></span><span>[+ pres_2025_runoff_poll_a +]%</span><span>±1.6</span></div>
<div class="poll-row"><span><b>[+ pres_2025_runoff_b_name +]</b></span><span>[+ pres_2025_runoff_poll_b +]%</span><span>±1.6</span></div>
</div>

Our line is **[+ pres_2025_transfer_strategy +]**; the campaign we are asking
our voters to reach is **[+ pres_2025_runoff_support_name +]**.
[? if pres_2025_runoff_poll_margin < 2: **[+ pres_2025_runoff_poll_leader +]**
leads by **[+ pres_2025_runoff_poll_margin +]** points, which is inside the
margin and therefore not a lead.?][? if pres_2025_runoff_poll_margin >= 2:
**[+ pres_2025_runoff_poll_leader +]** leads by **[+
pres_2025_runoff_poll_margin +]** points, which is outside the margin and
still not a result.?]

One operation remains. It is chosen before the count and cannot be revised
after it.

- @pres25_push_protect
- @pres25_push_ground
- @pres25_push_contrast

@pres25_push_protect
title: Protect the agreements and promise nothing further
subtitle: A small disciplined gain and the lowest factional risk.
on-arrival: pres_2025_runoff_final_push = "Held the line"; pres_2025_runoff_push_bonus = 0.15; party_unity += 2; news_headline = "The Left closes the runoff on the terms it already agreed"

Nothing new is offered, promised or leaked. Every appearance repeats the text
already published, every organiser works the list already drawn up, and the
leadership spends the fortnight refusing to improve on its own position under
pressure from people who will not be responsible for keeping it.

It is the least exciting fortnight available and the only one that leaves no
debts behind on the second of June.

- @presidential_runoff_2025: The second ballot

@pres25_push_ground
title: Empty the offices into election-day turnout
subtitle: The strongest mobilisation on the board; organisers will be finished afterwards.
on-arrival: pres_2025_runoff_final_push = "Ground operation"; pres_2025_runoff_push_bonus = 0.5; pres_2025_transfer_strength += 0.1; turnout_readiness += 2; organiser_energy -= 6; news_headline = "The Left puts every remaining organiser on runoff turnout"

Every district office closes and reopens as a phone bank. Regional staff sleep
on floors, the youth organisation runs transport, and the whole apparatus
spends fourteen days doing the one thing that cannot be bought late: reminding
people who already agree with us that the date is the first of June.

By the Monday after the count there will be nobody left standing in the party
who is capable of organising anything for a month. That is a problem for June.

- @presidential_runoff_2025: The second ballot

@pres25_push_contrast
title: Release a hard final contrast campaign
subtitle: Maximum reach, maximum backlash, and a fortnight that cannot be taken back.
on-arrival: pres_2025_runoff_final_push = "Final contrast"; pres_2025_runoff_push_bonus = 0.65; media_capacity += 2; public_trust -= 1; party_unity -= 3; progressives_dissent += 2; news_headline = "A hard final contrast campaign dominates the last week before the runoff"

The material is accurate, unpleasant and everywhere: what the other finalist has
signed, said, promised and abandoned, cut to fifteen seconds and paid into every
feed the campaign can reach. It works the way these things work — not by
persuading anyone, but by making the alternative unbearable to a few hundred
thousand people who might otherwise have stayed home.

Two of our own MPs decline to share it. The complaint inside the party is not
that it is untrue; it is that a fortnight of this is how a Left campaign learns
to sound like everybody else.

- @presidential_runoff_2025: The second ballot

@presidential_runoff_2025
# prose-audit: Grade B — The calculated count is now followed by candidate-specific consequences for Lewica and the constitutional order; only the hidden simulation retains model terminology.
title: Presidential election — runoff
subtitle: Endorsements matter, but voters do not transfer automatically
